{#
  Nav rail — one of the site's two selection components, for moving
  between the fixed sub-pages or views of one page: the finance pages,
  the rental management pages, the groups tabs, a status filter declared
  in code. The set is small, fixed and short-labelled.

  The other one is partials/select_bar.html.twig, for picking a piece of
  data. The rule, when a new call site appears
  (docs/module-development.md carries it too):

      fixed set, declared in code, short labels  → nav rail
      open-ended set, coming from the database   → select bar

  They share no markup and no JS. A nav rail never opens a panel; a
  select bar never renders chips. Do not add a use-case-specific
  parameter to either (no `is_section`, no `for_finance`) — a call site
  that seems to need one is using the wrong component.

  Shape: one horizontally-scrollable row of underlined tabs. Never
  wrapped, never folded, nothing hidden behind a "+N", and the selected
  tab is scrolled into view by public/assets/js/nav-rail.js. Every item
  is rendered server-side, so the rail is complete and operable with no
  JS at all — the script only centres the current tab.

  Bootstrap's own `nav nav-underline` + `flex-nowrap` + `overflow-auto`,
  no custom component (AGENTS.md: never duplicate a Bootstrap component).
  This is a deliberate, approved partial reversal of UX-convergence
  decision #4 ("nav-pills → chips"): chips remain wrong for
  sub-navigation, but so were pills — see design.md §7.6.

  Usage:
  {% include 'partials/nav_rail.html.twig' with {
      picker_id: 'finance-page-picker',
      items: [
          { id: '/finance', label: 'Tableau de bord', selected: true },
          { id: '/finance/movements', label: 'Mouvements', selected: false }
      ],
      base_url: '',
      extra_query: '?account_id=3'
  } %}

  Parameters:
  - `picker_id`  — unique per instance on a page; it is the DOM id.
  - `items`      — `id` and `label` required, `selected`, `color`,
                   `icon` and `align_end` optional. The link is
                   `{{ base_url }}{{ item.id }}{{ extra_query }}`, so an
                   `id` may equally be a numeric id or a full path.
                   `align_end` pushes an item — and everything after it —
                   to the far end of the rail. It exists for the one item
                   that LEAVES the page the rail navigates: the news
                   form's « Finance » tab opens another module, and a tab
                   sitting flush against its siblings reads as a fifth
                   view of the same page. Layout only, and deliberately
                   generic — it names no module and no use case. When the
                   rail overflows, the auto margin collapses to nothing
                   and the row simply scrolls, as before.
  - `base_url` / `extra_query` — both optional, both default to ''.
  - `aria_label` — names the <nav> landmark; defaults to 'Navigation'.

  `aria-current="page"` marks the selected tab. Selection is a plain
  <a href> — no JS, no client-side state.
#}
{% if items|length > 0 %}
    <nav class="nav-rail" id="{{ picker_id }}" aria-label="{{ aria_label|default('Navigation') }}">
        <ul class="nav nav-underline flex-nowrap overflow-auto">
            {% for item in items %}
                {% set selected = item.selected is defined and item.selected %}
                <li class="nav-item{{ item.align_end is defined and item.align_end ? ' ms-auto' : '' }}">
                    <a href="{{ base_url|default('') }}{{ item.id }}{{ extra_query|default('') }}"
                       class="nav-link tap-target text-nowrap d-inline-flex align-items-center gap-1{{ selected ? ' active' : '' }}"
                       data-id="{{ item.id }}"
                       data-selected="{{ selected ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
                       {{ selected ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' }}>
                        {% if item.color is defined and item.color %}
                            <span class="d-inline-block rounded-circle flex-shrink-0" style="width:0.55rem;height:0.55rem;background-color:{{ item.color }};"></span>
                        {% endif %}
                        {% if item.icon is defined and item.icon %}
                            <i class="bi {{ item.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        {% endif %}
                        <span>{{ item.label }}</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
        </ul>
    </nav>
{% endif %}
